  "numerical_failure": {
    "category": "correctness",
    "affected_api": "cvxpy.reductions.eliminate_pwl.canonicalizers.sum_largest_canon.sum_largest_canon",
    "observed_behavior": "The DNLP warm-start in sum_largest_canon sets the threshold q from the k smallest elements (sorted_indices[:k]) instead of the k-th largest, and initializes t only for the top-k elements. Middle elements with x_i > q then violate x_i <= t_i + q, giving an infeasible warm start. For x=[1,10,6,8,3,9] with k=2, the original code gives q=max of the two smallest values (1 and 3), i.e. q=3, which follows from the described formula; middle elements 6 and 8 violate the constraint.",
    "expected_behavior": "q should be min(x[top-k]), the k-th largest element, and t should be max(0, x - q) for all elements, so that x_i <= t_i + q and t_i >= 0 hold for every i. For x=[1,10,6,8,3,9] with k=2, q=9",
    "manifestation": "wrong_value",
    "trigger_conditions": [
      "x.value is not None when sum_largest_canon runs (warm-start branch)",
      "elements between q and the top-k (middle elements where x_i > q) exist",
      "DNLP warm-start path used with an NLP solver such as IPOPT or Knitro"
    ],
    "input_output_examples": [],
    "oracle_clues": [
      "regression tests check that q and t are computed correctly",
      "tests assert constraints x_i <= t_i + q and t_i >= 0 hold for all elements, including middle elements",
      "commit message says q should be min(x[top-k]) and t = max(0, x - q) for all elements"
    ]
  },
